.docname {External Simulators}
.include {docs}

CrowdVision ships three simulators that exist **outside the main application cluster**. They are not microservices — they are test stand-ins for systems that in production would be real external hardware or third-party services.

| Simulator | Location | Simulates |
|---|---|---|
| **Sensor Simulator** | `simulators/sensor-simulator/` | Thermostats (`temperature`) and people counters (`peopleCount`) |
| **Air Quality Simulator** | `simulators/aq-simulator/` | Air-quality stations (`airQuality`) |
| **AP Simulator** | `simulators/ap-simulator/` | Routers (`router`), as OpenWrt access points `devices/ap-collector` polls |

---

## Architectural Decision: Keep Simulators External

**Decision:** Simulators are never included in Kubernetes manifests and are not part of the production system definition.

**Why:** In production, CrowdVision receives data from physical IoT sensors and third-party AQ stations. Those devices are external — they know nothing about the cluster; they just POST to public API endpoints. The simulators model exactly this:

- The K8s manifests describe only the real system — no test tooling bleeds into production configs.
- Each simulator posts readings the same way real hardware would: to `/telemetry/ingest`, signed.
- Telemetry commands a simulator the way it commands a device's action endpoint: by a URL from its own configuration (`SIMULATORS`). k8s sets none, so there a start answers `404`.

---

## What Gets Simulated

**Exactly the sensors placed in the digital twin.** A simulator starts empty and simulates only what telemetry tells it at start.

| Device kind | Simulator | Reading per sensor per tick |
|---|---|---|
| `temperature` | sensor-simulator | one `temperature`, in the sensor's room |
| `peopleCount` | sensor-simulator | one `peopleCount`, in the sensor's room |
| `airQuality` | aq-simulator | one `airQuality`, in the sensor's room, with its own drifting state |
| `router` | ap-simulator | none: serves `(mac, rssi)` station lists over ubus; ap-collector turns them into `totalDeviceCount` / `ratioDeviceCount` |

- **Outdoor sensors** (no room) are never simulated: readings are room-keyed.
- **Two sensors of one kind in one room** give two readings for that room each tick.
- **Sync happens only at start.** Sensors added, moved or removed during a run are simulated from the next start.

---

## How It Works

1. The dashboard's **Start simulator** calls `PUT /telemetry/simulation/buildings/{id}` (editing role required).
2. Telemetry reads the building's sensors and splits them by device kind, using `SIMULATORS`.
3. Telemetry POSTs each simulator `/control/start` with its share — an empty list when it has none, which stops that building.
4. Each simulator ticks every 10 s and POSTs one signed batch to its `TELEMETRY_INGEST_URL`.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant UI as Dashboard (browser)
        participant T as telemetry
        participant S as sensor-simulator
        participant A as aq-simulator
        participant P as ap-simulator
        participant C as ap-collector

        UI->>T: PUT /telemetry/simulation/buildings/b1
        T->>T: read b1's sensors, split by kind
        par
            T->>S: POST /control/start {b1, [thermostats, people counters]}
        and
            T->>A: POST /control/start {b1, [air-quality stations]}
        and
            T->>P: POST /control/start {b1, [routers], rooms?}
        end
        T-->>UI: 204

        loop Every 10 seconds
            S->>T: POST TELEMETRY_INGEST_URL (signed batch)
            A->>T: POST TELEMETRY_INGEST_URL (signed batch)
            C->>P: POST /<sensorId>/ubus (station list per AP)
            C->>T: POST TELEMETRY_INGEST_URL (signed batch)
        end

**Stop** (`DELETE`) tells every simulator to drop the building. **Status** (`GET`) is `running` when any simulator runs it. The browser never calls a simulator.

---

## Control API

Called by telemetry only. The start body is pinned by `schemas/fixtures/simulation-start.json`, which every simulator's tests assert.

| Method | Endpoint | Body | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| `POST` | `/control/start` | `{ buildingId, sensors: [{ sensorId, sensorType, roomId, position? }], rooms? }` | Replaces what the building simulates. Empty `sensors` stops it. Geometry is optional; a simulator that does not need it ignores it. Any other key (e.g. the old `targetUrl`) is refused. |
| `POST` | `/control/stop` | `{ buildingId }` | Stops the building. Stopping one it never simulated succeeds. |
| `GET` | `/control/status?buildingId=X` | — | `{ isRunning }` |

---

## AP Simulator

Fakes the routers, not their readings: the collector stays the only thing that turns stations into counts, so its zone logic runs on simulated data too.

- **One AP per router**, served at `/<sensorId>/ubus` (credentials `collector`/`collector`). A restart drops the building's sessions; the collector logs in again.
- **Placement**: a placed router sits at its twin `position`; an unplaced one at its room's centre, 0.3 m under the ceiling.
- **No `rooms`** in the body: each router room becomes an 8 × 8 m box in a row, and twin positions are ignored — they have nothing to be measured against.
- **People**: `AP_SIM_DEVICES_PER_ROOM` (4) phones per room, over every room, including rooms without a router. Each walks 2–4 random stops, holds 2–15 min, and is in the building about 08:30–17:30 local time. Seeded by `buildingId`: a restart walks the same people.
- **Signal**: log-distance path loss over 3D distance (floors attenuate by height), 2 dB noise, −85 dBm cutoff. Every AP in range hears a phone, each at its own strength.
- **ap-collector must list the same APs** to produce readings. Today its list is static (`collector.dev.json`), so the dashboard's start alone yields no router readings yet.
- The preset topology (`AP_SIM_SCENARIO`) keeps serving the static collector config. `/debug/status`, `/debug/scenario`, `/debug/kill/{ap}`, `/debug/revive/{ap}`, `/debug/ground-truth` inspect and drive it.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `TELEMETRY_INGEST_URL` | `http://gateway/telemetry/ingest` | Where readings go. Set by the simulator's own environment, never by a caller. |
| `TELEMETRY_INGEST_SECRET` | *(empty)* | HMAC key for `X-Signature`; must match telemetry's. |

---

## Running the Simulators

Both are part of the Docker Compose stack and start with `just stack dev`:

```bash
just stack dev --no-simulators   # skip both simulators
just stack dev aq                # skip only the AQ simulator
```

Standalone, against a cluster on the host (the images are built and pushed to GHCR by CD):

```bash
docker run -p 3001:3000 --add-host host.docker.internal:host-gateway \
  -e TELEMETRY_INGEST_URL=http://host.docker.internal/telemetry/ingest \
  -e TELEMETRY_INGEST_SECRET=<secret> \
  ghcr.io/nickghignatti/crowdvision-simulator:latest
```

Inside a container `localhost` is the container itself, so the ingest URL must name the host. `--add-host` is needed on Linux only; Docker Desktop resolves `host.docker.internal` by itself. Telemetry must then list the simulator's URL in `SIMULATORS` to start it.

---

## Simulation Parameters

Fixed in `simulatorService.ts`; change them by editing the service and rebuilding the image.

| Parameter | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `delay` | `10000 ms` | Interval between batches. |
| `peopleCountRange` | `[0, 50]` | Random headcount per people counter per tick. |
| `temperatureRange` | `[18.0, 30.0]` | Random °C per thermostat per tick, 2 decimals. |

The air-quality simulator's physics, scenarios and sensor-error model live in `simulators/aq-simulator/` (`physics.py`, `scenarios.py`, `errors.py`); its tick interval defaults to 10 s.
